TITLE:: RLRelation
summary:: A relation between two finite lists of objects, with values in a quantale
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLQuantale, Classes/RLCategory, Classes/RLRelationContext, Classes/RLGraph, Classes/RLTransform, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
Many musical relations are not functions: an augmented triad is one semitone from six triads, a triad shares two tones with three others. A relation says, for each object of a strong::source:: list and each object of a strong::target:: list, whether the first is related to the second, and with which value.

The two lists are Arrays of objects (chords, pitch classes, anything with code::==:: and code::hash::). code::rows[i][j]:: is the value for code::source[i]:: and code::target[j]::, the quantale's zero when they are not related; a boolean relation, the default, holds 0 and 1 (link::Classes/RLQuantale::).

Relations compose with code::<>::, strong::right to left::: code::(s <> r):: relates x to z when r relates x to some y and s relates that y to z, with the value "join over y of s(y, z) * r(x, y)" in the quantale. The k-th power of a relation relates x to y exactly when a walk of k steps joins them.

A relation is a value: equal to another with the same lists, quantale and values, whatever its label.

code::
~space = RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(12));
~c = ~space.major(0);
~near = RLRelation.voiceMoves(~space.all, [1]);     // one voice moves by one step
~near.images(~c);                                   // [C minor, E minor]
(~near <> ~near).images(~c);                        // [C major, E major, Ab major]
~near.repeated(3).relates(~c, ~space.minor(8));     // true: three steps to the hexatonic pole
~near.isFunction;                                   // false

// a relation between two lists: the tones of the chords
~in = RLRelation.between(RLEDO(12).pcs, ~space.all, { |pc, chord| chord.pcs.includes(pc) });
~in.images(0).size;                                 // 6: C belongs to six triads
(~in <> ~in.converse).relates(~c, ~space.major(7)); // true: C major and G major share a tone

// costs: the closure of a relation of lawvere costs is a distance
~cost = RLRelation.commonTones(~space.all, 2).valued(RLQuantale.lawvere, { |x, y| RLVoiceLeading.stepsBetween(x, y) });
~cost.at(~c, ~space.minor(9));                      // 2.0: R moves a voice by a whole tone
~cost.closure.at(~c, ~space.major(1));              // 5.0: C major to C# major along P, L, R
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: source
argument:: target
Two Arrays of objects.
argument:: rows
One Array per source object, one value per target object (numbers, or true, false, nil).
argument:: quantale
link::Classes/RLQuantale#*boolean:: when nil.
argument:: label

METHOD:: between
From a rule: code::func.(x, y):: answers a value, true, false or nil for each pair.

METHOD:: on
The same on one list, as source and target.

METHOD:: fromPairs
From an Array of code::[x, y]:: or code::[x, y, value]::.

METHOD:: identity
Each object related to itself.

METHOD:: ofFunction
A function as a relation: x is related to code::func.(x)::, which must be one of the objects.

METHOD:: voiceMoves
Chords related by a voice leading whose moves are these numbers of steps, the other voices staying: code::[1]:: one voice by one step, code::[1, 1]:: two voices by one step each. The chords belong to one link::Classes/RLEDO::. In 12 steps code::[1]:: is "P or L" and code::[2]:: is R; in 19 steps P, L, R are code::[1]::, code::[2]::, code::[3]::; in 31, code::[2]::, code::[3]::, code::[5]::.
argument:: chords
argument:: moves
argument:: quantale
argument:: label

METHOD:: pmn
Douthett and Steinbach's P(m, n): m voices by one step and n by two.

METHOD:: commonTones
Chords that share exactly k tones and are not the same chord.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: source
METHOD:: target
METHOD:: rows
METHOD:: quantale
METHOD:: label

METHOD:: at
The value at (x, y).

METHOD:: relates
Whether x is related to y.

METHOD:: images
The objects x is related to, in the order of the target.

METHOD:: preimages
The objects related to y.

METHOD:: value
The one image of x. An Error when x has none or several: a relation is a function only where it has one image.

METHOD:: <>
This after that. An Error when this does not start on the list where that ends, or when the quantales differ.

METHOD:: follows
Whether this can come after that.

METHOD:: repeated
n times itself.

METHOD:: converse
The same pairs read backwards.

METHOD:: |
The join of two relations, value by value (their union).

METHOD:: without
This where that has nothing.

METHOD:: <=
Whether this is included in that: every value below that's.

METHOD:: valued
The same pairs with other values, in another quantale.
argument:: newQuantale
argument:: func
code::func.(x, y, value):: for each related pair.

METHOD:: closure
The join of the identity and of every power: with Lawvere costs, the least cost of a walk between two objects (inf when none joins them); with a boolean relation, whether a walk joins them.

METHOD:: pairs
The related pairs, code::[x, y, value]:: each.

METHOD:: pairCount
METHOD:: degrees
How many pairs; how many images each source object has.

METHOD:: isTotal
METHOD:: isFunction
METHOD:: isBijection
METHOD:: isSymmetric
METHOD:: isIdentity
Every object has an image; exactly one; one to one; the same read backwards; each object related to itself only.

METHOD:: sourceIdentity
METHOD:: targetIdentity
The identity on each list (what an link::Classes/RLCategory:: asks of its arrows).
